  "name": "Question 3",
  "problem": "Which of the following shaded areas describes the region bounded by the inequality $y<-3x-4?$",
  "worked_solution": "First, we draw the corresponding straight line  $y=-3x-4.$ Since the inequality involves a 'strictly less than' sign, the line  $y=-3x-4$ itself is *not* part of the solution. Therefore, we draw a **dashed line.**\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14274623/assets/4fa83a1bc9fcbfc2e4a5a20896f54091fcd782993148d55cff584ce36ffce610.png)\n\nThen, we must decide whether to shade above or below the line. The inequality $y<-3x-4$ refers to the $y$-values that are *less than* those on the line $y=-3x-4,$ so we shade *below* the line. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14274623/assets/fc5391f466c20e3a6c025f75be34a3de90c354d26f04cd5df8d505218052a730.png)\n\nLet's now check that we've shaded the correct part. First, we consider a point in the shaded region, say $(-3,0).$  \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14274623/assets/0f79d4b33c554c5628f80963f560f29ea2275a6d8ec957e1d2175c69209c74ab.png)\n\n \n\nWhen we substitute the coordinates of this point into our inequality, we get a true statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & <-3x-4 \\\\ (0) & <-3(-3)-4 \\\\ 0 & <5\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThis indicates that we have shaded the correct region.",
